mode: "100644"
text: "# Formats & Sound\n\nPick the format that fits the idea and the creator's comfort, then decide the sound. Both are\nTikTok-specific choices that a generic \"video script\" misses.\n\n## TikTok formats\n\n- **Talking head** — you, to camera, with energy. The TikTok default; rewards personality. Best for\n  opinions, tips, storytime, hot takes.\n- **Storytime** — a narrative with tension and a payoff (\"we deleted our most-requested feature.\n  here's what happened\"). High completion when the hook promises a real resolution.\n- **POV / skit** — \"POV: you're the founder who…\". Relatable, native, shareable.\n- **Tutorial / how-to** — screen-record or demo; strong for saves and search. Number the steps.\n- **Listicle** — \"3 tools that…\". Fast, dense, rewatchable; great loop fuel.\n- **Green-screen explainer** — talk over an article/screenshot/chart. Easy, native, credible.\n- **Stitch / Duet reaction** — react to or extend another video; piggybacks reach + adds your POV.\n- **Faceless / voiceover** — b-roll or AI visuals + voiceover, no on-camera presence. For niches,\n  shy creators, or scaled output (this is where the Veo pack earns its place — see\n  `veo-prompt-pack.md`).\n\nMatch format to the message and to who's making it: don't script a dance skit for someone who won't\nperform it, or a faceless voiceover when a founder's face would build more trust.\n\n## Sound strategy\n\nSound is part of TikTok discovery, not decoration. Three choices:\n\n1. **Ride a trending sound** — a rising sound can give early algorithmic lift and signals \"native.\"\n   Use when it **genuinely fits** the mood or format. Catching a sound early (on the way up) beats a\n   played-out one.\n2. **Original audio** — your own talking/voiceover. Best for storytime, tutorials, opinions — and a\n   great original audio can itself spread (others use it). Most brand/founder content lives here.\n3. **Music bed** — licensed/commercial-safe track for vibe under a voiceover. Mind **commercial\n   sound licensing** for brand accounts (the trending-but-unlicensed song can be a rights problem;\n   use TikTok's commercial library or original audio).\n\n### The fit test — don't force a trend\n\nA trend or sound must serve the message and suit the audience. Forcing the current dance sound onto\na B2B compliance explainer reads as try-hard and hurts trust. If a trend doesn't fit: use original\naudio, or find a format-level trend (a structure, a meme template) that does. For live trend\nresearch and timing, hand off to **`trend-jacking`** — this skill rides a sound where it fits; it\ndoesn't chase the trend feed.\n\n## The sound *as* the hook\n\nSometimes the sound is the hook — a recognizable audio drop, a beat switch synced to your cut, a\nsound everyone associates with a format. When you use a trending sound, sync your hook frame and\ncuts to its beats; the familiarity does some of the scroll-stopping for you.\n\n## Output note\n\nIn the production doc, name the **sound choice** (trending / original / music) and, when it matters,\nthe **beat sync** (which cut lands on which beat). That's the TikTok-native detail a generic script\nleaves out.\n"
